273	
274	## What makes System One composable
275	
276	<Columns cols={2}>
277	  <Card title="Structured" icon="braces">
278	    System One is type-safe by construction. Decisions and probabilities conform to the structured software types and JSON schema your code expects, so it never has to recover a value from generated prose.
279	  </Card>
280	
281	  <Card title="Parallel" icon="split">
282	    Questions are evaluated independently and in parallel. One primitive's result does not become hidden context that changes another primitive's result.
283	  </Card>
284	
285	  <Card title="Comparable" icon="arrow-up-down">
286	    Outputs are sortable and can drive smart `if` statements, thresholds, and comparisons.
287	  </Card>
288	
289	  <Card title="Fast" icon="gauge">
290	    Most queries complete in about 100 ms. System One is fast enough for real-time request paths and user interfaces.
291	  </Card>
292	
293	  <Card title="Calibrated confidence" icon="chart-no-axes-combined">
294	    [RLCD](/introduction/machine-learning-primer) communicates uncertainty through calibrated probabilities instead of tending toward overconfidence.
295	  </Card>
296	
297	  <Card title="Self-consistent" icon="repeat-2">
298	    System One is designed to return stable answers across repeated evaluations. See the [self-consistency cookbook](/cookbooks/consistency_noul_cookbook).
299	  </Card>
300	</Columns>
301	
302	Because every output is constrained to the supplied options, the model returns a full probability distribution over those options rather than inventing a value outside the schema. TypeSafe's target is a greater than 100× intelligence-to-speed-and-cost ratio; the underlying bet is that cheaper intelligence will create much more demand.
303	
304	## Design a System One workflow
305	
306	<Steps titleSize="h3">
307	  <Step title="Use code when you can">
308	    Keep deterministic work in code. It is reliable and cheap. Avoid agent `while` loops when a software workflow can express the same behavior.
309	
310	    <Accordion title="Example: keep deterministic rules in code">
311	      ```python theme={null}
312	      days_overdue = (today - invoice.due_date).days
313	
314	      if days_overdue > 30:
315	          route_to_collections(invoice)
316	      ```
317	    </Accordion>
318	
319	    Browse the [System One patterns](/patterns) for bounded ways to compose model decisions with code.
320	  </Step>
321	
322	  <Step title="Decompose the input state">
323	    Include only the context relevant to the current questions. This helps the model avoid distractions and context rot. Do not rely on knowledge stored in model weights when current information can come from your own knowledge base.
324	
325	    <Accordion title="Example: send only relevant context">
326	      <TypesafeExample
327	        title="request"
328	        display="request"
329	        example={{
330	      state: {
331	        ticket_message: 'My flight was cancelled. Can I get a refund?',
332	        refund_policy: 'Cancelled flights are eligible for a full refund.',
333	      },
334	      selectedModels: ['jev-latest'],
335	      questions: {
336	        policy_supports_refund: {
337	          type: 'noul',
338	          instructions:
339	            'Does the refund policy support the refund requested in the ticket?',
340	        },
341	      },
342	    }}
343	      />
344	    </Accordion>
345	  </Step>
346	
347	  <Step title="Use structure in the input state">
348	    Use nested JSON for the `state` and `questions` fields. Point questions at specific values when that removes ambiguity, and include the backtick characters around each path inside the question.
349	
350	    <Accordion title="Example: reference a nested value">
351	      Use a backticked dot-and-index path to point a question at a specific nested value, such as `support.tickets[0].message`.
352	
353	      <TypesafeExample
354	        title="request"
355	        display="request"
356	        example={{
357	      state: {
358	        support: {
359	          tickets: [
360	            { message: 'I was charged twice for order A-104.' },
361	            { message: 'How do I reset my password?' },
362	          ],
363	        },
364	        commerce: {
365	          orders: [
366	            {
367	              id: 'A-104',
368	              charges: [
369	                { amount_usd: 49, status: 'captured' },
370	                { amount_usd: 49, status: 'captured' },
371	              ],
372	            },
373	          ],
374	        },
375	        account: {
376	          security: {
377	            password_reset:
378	              'Email a reset link to the address on file.',
379	          },
380	        },
381	      },
382	      selectedModels: ['jev-latest'],
383	      questions: {
384	        duplicate_charge: {
385	          type: 'noul',
386	          instructions:
387	            'Do `support.tickets[0].message` and `commerce.orders[0].charges` indicate a duplicate charge?',
388	        },
389	        password_reset_supported: {
390	          type: 'noul',
391	          instructions:
392	            'Can `account.security.password_reset` resolve the request in `support.tickets[1].message`?',
393	        },
394	      },
395	    }}
396	      />
397	    </Accordion>
398	  </Step>
399	
400	  <Step title="Decompose the questions">
401	    Ask the most explicit, narrow, specific, atomic questions you can. Break down complex or ill-defined questions into separate questions that each evaluate one property.
402	
403	    <Info>
404	      This is probably the most important concept in this guide. Broad questions hide several judgments behind one answer. Atomic questions expose those judgments so you can inspect, tune, and combine them in code.
405	    </Info>
406	
