237	
238	TypeSafe's primitives are the small, typed building blocks you compose in code. They come in pairs: a question defines one judgment for a [System One model](/concepts/system-one) to make about a [state](/concepts/state), and its answer is the typed value that comes back. You compose the answers in your code to make decisions. There are three question types, each returning a different shape of answer.
239	
240	| Type                         | What it answers         | Returns                                          |
241	| ---------------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
242	| [Choice](/primitives/choice) | Which of these options? | `choice`, `probabilities`, `confidence`          |
243	| [Score](/primitives/score)   | Which level?            | `score`, `legend`, `probabilities`, `confidence` |
244	| [Noul](/primitives/noul)     | Is this true?           | `noul` (0 to 1)                                  |
245	
246	You can ask one question or send several together. Every question in a request sees the same state, is evaluated independently, and returns a typed answer under the ID you chose.
247	
248	## Ask for one snap judgment per question
249	
250	System One models are built for fast, focused judgments. Ask for a judgment a knowledgeable person makes in a second given the right context. "Does this message convey urgency?" is a good question. "Analyze this message and determine the best course of action" is not. That needs slow reasoning, and it is a signal to break the task into small questions and compose the answers in code.
251	
252	If the judgment you want depends on several independent factors, ask about each factor separately and combine the answers with your own logic. Instead of "rate this startup pitch", ask about market size, technical feasibility, and differentiation, then weight them in code based on their relative importance. When priorities shift, change the value of weights rather than rewriting a prompt. [Ask multiple questions together](#ask-multiple-questions-together) shows how to do this.
253	
254	## Define a question
255	
256	Every question has an ID, a `type`, and `instructions`. Choice and Score questions also take `criteria`, which define the options for a Choice question or the levels for a Score. Noul questions accept `criteria` as an optional clarification of what yes and no mean.
257	
258	* ID. The key you pick, such as `refund_requested`. It identifies the answer in the response.
259	* `type`. One of `choice`, `score`, or `noul`.
260	* `instructions`. The question you are asking about the state. This is where your evaluation logic goes. Write it as a clear, specific question, or as a statement for the model to judge.
261	* `criteria`. The possible answers: a map of options for a Choice question, an ordered list of levels for a Score, and an optional description of yes and no for a Noul. Each question type's page covers its shape.
262	
263	This question asks whether a customer requested a refund:
264	
265	```python theme={null}
266	from typesafe_sdk import Noul
267	
268	questions = {
269	    "refund_requested": Noul(
270	        instructions="Does the customer request a refund?",
271	    ),
272	}
273	```
274	
275	<Tip>
276	  Question IDs are for your code. They are not sent to the model. Write the complete question in `instructions`, even when the ID seems self-explanatory.
277	</Tip>
278	
279	## Choose a question type
280	
281	Pick the type that matches the shape of the answer you need.
282	
283	* **Choice** fits when the answer is one of a known set of options with no order between them: routing a ticket to a department, classifying a document type, detecting a programming language. Give the full list of options, and add an `other` or `none of the above` option when the list might not cover every input.
284	
285	* **Score** fits when the answer falls on a spectrum and you can describe what each point on that spectrum means: bug severity, customer frustration, skill level. The levels are yours to define, and the model returns a position along them.
286	
287	* **Noul** fits a clean yes/no question where the probability itself is the useful signal: does this message report a bug, is the customer requesting a refund, does the resume mention distributed systems.
288	
289	<Note>
290	  Use Noul for a yes/no judgment and Score to measure a position on a spectrum. "Is this candidate strong in Python?" needs a clear definition of "strong". A Noul value of 0.5 means the model gives yes and no equal probability. It does not mean the candidate has a medium skill level. An unclear definition makes that probability hard to interpret.
291	
292	  If you want to measure skill level, use a Score with defined levels, such as no experience, some familiarity, daily use, and deep expertise. If you need a yes/no decision, define the condition clearly, such as "Does the resume state that the candidate has used Python at work?"
293	</Note>
294	
295	If two types both seem to fit, prefer the one whose answer your code can act on directly. A Choice between `refund`, `rebook`, and `information` maps straight onto three code paths. A Score of customer frustration maps onto a threshold. A Noul maps onto an `if`.
296	
297	## What comes back
298	
299	Answers are primitives too. Each question type returns a typed value that your code can compare, threshold, sort, pass into further logic, or put into the state of a follow-up request (see [When one question depends on another](#when-one-question-depends-on-another)).
300	
301	| Type   | Answer fields                                    | How to read it                                                                                                                                                       |
302	| ------ | ------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
303	| Choice | `choice`, `probabilities`, `confidence`          | `choice` is the selected option. `probabilities` is the distribution across every option. `confidence` summarizes how peaked that distribution is.                   |
304	| Score  | `score`, `legend`, `probabilities`, `confidence` | `score` is a position along your levels, and can fall between two of them. `legend` repeats the levels by number. `probabilities` is the distribution across levels. |
305	| Noul   | `noul`                                           | The probability that the answer is yes. Near 1 is a strong yes, near 0 a strong no, near 0.5 uncertain. Noul has no separate `confidence`.                           |
306	
307	Two properties of these answers make them composable:
308	
309	* **Every answer is constrained to the options you supplied.** The model returns a probability distribution over your options or levels, never a value outside them. Your code never has to recover a value from generated prose.
310	* **Every answer is independent.** One question's answer is not hidden context for another. You can add or remove questions without changing the others' results.
311	
312	[Confidence](/confidence) explains how `confidence` is derived from `probabilities` and how to use it to decide when to act automatically and when to escalate to a person.
313	
314	## Reference specific fields
315	
316	The content being evaluated, the [state](/concepts/state), is often a JSON object with several parts: a conversation, a record, a policy. When a question is about one of those parts, name it in the `instructions` with a dot-and-index path to its key, including the backticks. The model then knows which part of the state to judge.
317	
318	Take the support conversation from the State page:
319	
320	```json theme={null}
321	{
322	  "ticket": {
323	    "subject": "Duplicate charge",
324	    "messages": [
325	      {"from": "customer", "text": "I was charged twice for order A-104. Please refund the duplicate."},
326	      {"from": "support", "text": "We are checking the charges."}
327	    ]
328	  },
329	  "order": {
330	    "id": "A-104",
331	    "charges": [
332	      {"amount_usd": 49, "status": "captured"},
333	      {"amount_usd": 49, "status": "captured"}
334	    ]
335	  },
336	  "refund_policy": "Duplicate charges are eligible for a refund."
337	}
338	```
339	
340	These two questions point at the customer's message, the policy, and the charges by path:
341	
342	```python theme={null}
343	questions = {
344	    "refund_requested": {
345	        "type": "noul",
346	        "instructions": "Does `ticket.messages[0].text` request a refund?",
347	    },
348	    "policy_supports_refund": {
349	        "type": "noul",
350	        "instructions": (
351	            "Does `refund_policy` support the refund requested "
352	            "in `ticket.messages[0].text`, given `order.charges`?"
353	        ),
354	    },
355	}
356	```
357	
358	Explicit paths make it clear which parts of a structured state should inform each judgment. See [State](/concepts/state) for how to structure the input.
359	
360	## Ask multiple questions together
361	
362	Send every question that uses the same state in one request. You can mix question types freely. System One models evaluate every question in a request in parallel. Adding questions barely changes the response time and costs only the tokens for the extra questions, which are cheap. Asking a question you might not need is close to free.
363	
364	This request classifies a customer message, checks for urgency, and scores frustration all at once:
365	
366	<TypesafeExample
367	  example={{
368	state:
369	  "Our API integration started returning 500 errors on every request about 20 minutes ago, and we can't process any customer orders until this is fixed.",
370	questions: {
371	  department: {
372	    type: 'choice',
373	    instructions: 'Which team should handle this',
374	    criteria: {
375	      billing: 'Payment or subscription issues',
376	      technical: 'Bugs or integration problems',
377	      sales: 'Pricing or account questions',
378	    },
379	  },
380	  is_urgent: {
381	    type: 'noul',
382	    instructions: 'The message conveys urgency or time-sensitivity',
383	  },
384	  frustration: {
385	    type: 'score',
386	    instructions: 'How frustrated the customer appears',
387	    criteria: [
388	      'Calm, just stating facts',
389	      'Frustrated but civil',
390	      'Very angry, strong language',
391	    ],
392	  },
393	},
394	}}
395	/>
396	
397	Our [client SDKs](/sdk) provide typed questions and answers. In Python, pass a `questions` dictionary of `Choice`, `Noul`, and `Score` objects to `client.system_one(...)`. This request sends a ticket and a refund policy once and gets a typed answer for each question:
398	
399	```python theme={null}
400	from typesafe_sdk import Choice, Noul, Score, TypeSafeClient
401	
402	state = {
403	    "ticket_message": "My flight was cancelled. Can I get a refund?",
404	    "refund_policy": "Cancelled flights are eligible for a full refund.",
405	}
406	
407	with TypeSafeClient() as client:
408	    response = client.system_one(
409	        state=state,
410	        questions={
411	            "refund_requested": Noul(
412	                instructions="Does `ticket_message` request a refund?",
413	            ),
414	            "request_type": Choice(
415	                instructions="What is the main request in `ticket_message`?",
416	                criteria={
417	                    "refund": "The customer wants money returned.",
418	                    "rebooking": "The customer wants a replacement flight.",
419	                    "information": "The customer is asking for information only.",
420	                },
421	            ),
422	            "frustration": Score(
423	                instructions="How frustrated does the customer appear in `ticket_message`?",
424	                criteria=[
425	                    "Calm and neutral.",
426	                    "Concerned but civil.",
427	                    "Very angry or using strong language.",
428	                ],
429	            ),
430	        },
431	    )
432	
433	print(response.answers["refund_requested"].noul)
434	print(response.answers["request_type"].choice)
435	print(response.answers["frustration"].score)
436	```
437	
438	See [client SDKs](/sdk) for installation and usage in your language.
439	
440	### Ask speculative questions
441	
442	Ask every question your code might need, including ones whose answer only matters for some inputs, and let the code decide which answers to use. If a ticket turns out not to be a bug report, ignore the severity answer. We call this the [Speculative fan-out](/patterns/fan-out) pattern. The [Parallel questions cookbook](/cookbooks/parallel_questions) shows how batching 13 questions into one call is 11.5x cheaper and 9.6x faster than 13 separate calls, with no change in the answers.
443	
444	The number of questions in one request is limited only by the request's token budget, which the state and the questions share. The budget is around 32,000 tokens, roughly 150,000 characters of English text.
445	
446	<Tip>
447	  Coding agents fall into the one question per call habit more than people do. The [TypeSafe agent skill](/agent-skill#installation) tells your agent to put many questions in each call, including ones that only matter for some inputs.
448	</Tip>
449	
450	### Split a complex judgment into several questions
451	
452	A judgment that depends on several things is best split into one question per thing. Combine the answers in your code, giving each a weight for its relative importance. The weights are yours. When the combined result doesn't match what your team would decide, change them in code and run again. Adding questions barely changes the response time because they run in parallel within one request. The split costs a few extra question tokens.
453	
454	For example, ticket priority might be built from three Score questions: how severe the bug is, how frustrated the customer is, and how much the report gives an engineer to work with. The Score page walks through this request and the code that normalizes and weights the answers in [Splitting a complex judgment into several Scores](/primitives/score#splitting-a-complex-judgment-into-several-scores). This technique is called the [Composite scoring](/patterns/composite-scoring) pattern.
455	
456	### When one question depends on another
457	
458	Questions in the same request are independent: one answer does not become context for another question. If a later judgment depends on an earlier answer, make a second request in code. The dependency is real only when your code cannot build the second request until it has the first answer: it needs the answer to fetch more data for the state, to decide what the state is made of, or to pick the next question's options. Otherwise, ask the questions together and combine their answers in code.
459	
460	Two requests are the exception, not the rule. If the second request's questions could have been asked against the original state, ask them in the first request and let the code ignore the ones it doesn't need. Three cookbooks make a second request for a real reason. [Skill suggestion](/cookbooks/skill_suggestion) ranks 182 skills in one request, then fetches the full text of the top three and judges them again against that better evidence. [Structure recovery](/cookbooks/autoformat) asks whether each line break split a sentence, merges lines into blocks from those answers, then classifies the blocks, which did not exist until the first request had answered. [Hierarchical classification](/cookbooks/hierarchical_classification) uses each Choice answer to decide which options the next request offers.
461	
462	See [How to build with TypeSafe](/concepts/how-to-build-with-system-one) for guidance on breaking a workflow into focused judgments.
463	
464	## Next steps
465	
466	<Columns cols={3}>
467	  <Card title="Choice" href="/primitives/choice" icon="list">
468	    Pick one option from a fixed list.
469	  </Card>
470	
471	  <Card title="Score" href="/primitives/score" icon="gauge">
472	    Rate the state along ordered levels.
473	  </Card>
474	
475	  <Card title="Noul" href="/primitives/noul" icon="circle-check">
476	    Get the probability that a statement is true.
477	  </Card>
478	</Columns>
479	
480	To see how these compose into system architectures, head to [Patterns](/patterns).
