725	Examples steer the model, and they only help when they look like your real inputs. The table below is the opening Safari report with three different sets of level objects:
726	
727	| Level description                                                                                            | `score` | `confidence` |
728	| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------- | ------------ |
729	| plain string: no object with examples                                                                        | 1.30    | 0.54         |
730	| Added examples array with useful example: "export fails in one browser but works in another"                 | 1.07    | 0.90         |
731	| Added examples array with example unrelated to browsers: "search fails, but browsing categories still works" | 1.28    | 0.57         |
732	
733	In this comparison, the matching example concentrates more probability on one level. The unrelated example changes the result only slightly compared with plain strings. Higher confidence does not establish which answer is correct. Choose examples with known expected levels, then test the revised descriptions on separate inputs before keeping them.
